Summary:

There are a few calls to `pushGlobal()` that run for every tensor, which does a heap allocation and a hash of the key, and at scale with lots and lots of calls it adds up. We can cache the id after the first tensor and save on all the subsequent calls. The pickled bytes are identical.

Why only these callers: because they run for every regular tensor which scales poorly.

Benchmark:

```
c10::List<at::Tensor> list;
for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i)
  list.push_back(torch::randn(8));
c10::IValue value(list);

// tensor table
std::vector<at::Tensor> table;
auto blob = torch::jit::pickle(value, &table);  // timed

// literal tensors (as in RPC wireSerialize)
std::string out;
torch::jit::Pickler p([&](const char* b, size_t len) { out.append(b, len); });
p.protocol(); p.pushIValue(value); p.stop();  // timed
```

```
  tensor table  literal tensors
tensors  before  after  before  after
  10  1.52 us  1.20 us  3.83 us  2.77 us
  100  13.2 us  9.42 us  36.2 us  24.7 us
  1000  128 us  88.7 us  357 us  233 us
```

Authored with an AI assistant (Claude Code).

Test Plan:
buck2 test //caffe2/test/cpp/api:jit //caffe2/torch/fb/distributed/wireSerializer/test:WireSerializerTest //caffe2/torch/fb/distributed/wireSerializer/test:DictWireSerializerHelperTest

