I was exploring how to focus an input element automatically when a component mounts.

The documentation explains how to use useRef to access DOM elements, but it would also be helpful to include an example using callback refs.

Example:

function Chat() {
  const inputRef = (element) => {
  element?.focus();
  };

  return (
  <input
  ref={inputRef}
  placeholder="Type something..."
  />
  );
}

Expected explanation:

React calls the callback ref when the DOM element is attached.

The DOM element is passed as an argument to the callback.

We can directly call DOM methods such as .focus() inside the callback.

React calls the callback with null when the element is detached (for the usual callback-ref pattern).

It would be helpful to include this approach alongside the useRef and useEffect example to demonstrate how callback refs can be used for DOM interactions.

**React version:** 19.3.0-canary-f93b9fd4-20251217 (as vendored by Next.js 16.1.2); the same code is on `main`.

**Steps to reproduce**

```js
// server
renderToString(<div><b /><Leaf /></div>)  // Leaf renders <span id={useId()} />
// client
const LazyLeaf = lazy(() => Promise.resolve({ default: Leaf }))
startTransition(() => hydrateRoot(container, <div><b /><LazyLeaf /></div>))
```

**Current behavior:** "A tree hydrated but some attributes … didn't match": server `id="_R_2_"`, client `_R_0_`.

**Expected behavior:** the ids match.

**Cause:** `LazyLeaf` is a forked child (it has a sibling), so `beginWork` pushes its tree id when `isHydrating && (flags & Forked)`. It suspends in `resolveLazy`; the concurrent work loop waits and then calls `replaySuspendedUnitOfWork` → `replayBeginWork`, default branch: `unwindInterruptedWork` pops the tree context, then `resetWorkInProgress` runs `flags &= StaticMask | Placement`, which clears `Forked`. `beginWork` then skips the push, so every `useId` below is computed one level short.

In Next.js App Router this happens to a route's layout slot (`[segment scripts, layout output]`) whenever its payload isn't ready during hydration, in roughly 1 in 7 cold loads on our app, which shifts every `useId` under the layout.

**Fix that works for us:** keep `Forked` across the reset in that branch:

```js
default: {
  const forked = unitOfWork.flags & Forked;
  unwindInterruptedWork(current, unitOfWork, workInProgressRootRenderLanes);
  unitOfWork = workInProgress = resetWorkInProgress(unitOfWork, entangledRenderLanes);
  unitOfWork.flags |= forked;
  next = beginWork(current, unitOfWork, entangledRenderLanes);
}
```

Measured: 12 of 80 loads mismatched before, 0 of 80 after.

React versions: **19.3.1** (latest stable) and **19.3.0-canary-dc9aed36-20261027** (latest canary), with matching `react-dom`. Both were checked against the public npm dist-tags on October 29, 2026.

A synchronous `useSyncExternalStore` notification during one root's render can leave context defaults corrupted in an unrelated root when the subscribed root has a commit waiting for a stylesheet.

The reproduction below uses only React and React DOM at runtime, plus Vite to serve/build it. There is no third-party store library, application code, or instrumentation of React internals.

## Steps To Reproduce

1. Put the four files below in an empty directory, then run `npm install` and `npm run dev`.
2. Open the URL printed by Vite and click **Synchronous notification** (`?mode=sync`).
3. Root A subscribes to an external store and starts an update containing a slow `<link rel="stylesheet" precedence="repro">`. The server delays that stylesheet for 30 seconds, leaving the commit pending.
4. While A is waiting, root B renders providers for two contexts. A child synchronously notifies A's external-store subscription during B's render.
5. Root C renders without a provider and reads a context whose default is `{enabled: true}`.
6. Compare the **Deferred notification** and **Synchronous notification without stylesheet** links. Each link reloads the document so it starts with fresh context instances.

For the production build, run `npm run build` followed by `npm run preview`, then use the same links.

Link to code example: the complete, self-contained example is inline below.

<details>
<summary>Complete reproduction files</summary>

### package.json

```json
{
  "name": "react-cross-root-context-repro",
  "private": true,
  "type": "module",
  "scripts": {
  "dev": "vite --host 127.0.0.1",
  "build": "vite build",
  "preview": "vite preview --host 127.0.0.1"
  },
  "dependencies": {
  "react": "19.3.1",
  "react-dom": "19.3.1"
  },
  "devDependencies": {
  "vite": "8.3.3"
  }
}
```

### index.html

```html
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8" />
  <title>Cross-root context reproduction</title>
  </head>
  <body>
  <a href="?mode=sync">Synchronous notification</a>
  <a href="?mode=deferred">Deferred notification</a>
  <a href="?mode=sync&css=off">Synchronous notification without stylesheet</a>
  <div id="a"></div>
  <div id="b"></div>
  <div id="c"></div>
  <pre id="log"></pre>
  <script type="module" src="/main.js"></script>
  </body>
</html>
```

### vite.config.mjs

```js
import {defineConfig} from 'vite'

function slowStylesheet(server) {
  server.middlewares.use('/slow.css', (_request, response) => {
  const timer = setTimeout(() => {
  response.writeHead(200, {'Content-Type': 'text/css', 'Cache-Control': 'no-store'})
  response.end('p { color: black }')
  }, 30_000)
  response.once('close', () => clearTimeout(timer))
  })
}

export default defineConfig({
  plugins: [{
  name: 'slow-stylesheet',
  configureServer: slowStylesheet,
  configurePreviewServer: slowStylesheet,
  }],
})
```

### main.js

```js
import {createContext, createElement as h, useContext, useState, useSyncExternalStore, version} from 'react'
import {flushSync} from 'react-dom'
import {createRoot} from 'react-dom/client'

const params = new URLSearchParams(location.search)
const mode = params.get('mode')
const events = []
const reads = {client: [], flags: []}
const errors = []
function log(message) {
  events.push(message)
  document.querySelector('#log').textContent = events.join('\n')
}

let snapshot = 0
const listeners = new Set()
const subscribe = listener => {
  listeners.add(listener)
  return () => listeners.delete(listener)
}
function notify() {
  snapshot++
  listeners.forEach(listener => listener())
}

const Flags = createContext({enabled: true})
const Client = createContext(undefined)

let suspendCommit = () => {}
function A() {
  const count = useSyncExternalStore(subscribe, () => snapshot)
  const [pending, setPending] = useState(false)
  suspendCommit = () => setPending(true)
  return h('p', null, `Root A: snapshot ${count}`,
  pending && params.get('css') !== 'off' && h('link', {
  rel: 'stylesheet',
  href: '/slow.css',
  precedence: 'repro',
  }),
  )
}

let notified = false
function Notify() {
  if (!notified) {
  notified = true
  if (mode === 'deferred') setTimeout(notify, 0)
  else notify()
  }
  return null
}
function ReadClient() {
  const value = useContext(Client)
  reads.client.push(value)
  log(`B client: ${JSON.stringify(value)} (provided "provided-client")`)
  return h('p', null, `Root B: ${String(value)}`)
}
function C() {
  const value = useContext(Flags)
  reads.flags.push(value)
  log(`C flags: ${JSON.stringify(value)} (default {"enabled":true})`)
  return h('p', null, `Root C: ${JSON.stringify(value)}`)
}

function recordError(error) {
  errors.push(error.message)
  log(`error: ${error.message}`)
}
const options = {onUncaughtError: recordError, onRecoverableError: recordError}
const a = createRoot(document.querySelector('#a'), options)
const b = createRoot(document.querySelector('#b'), options)
const c = createRoot(document.querySelector('#c'), options)
window.repro = {events, reads, errors, version, build: import.meta.env.MODE}

flushSync(() => a.render(h(A)))
log(`React ${version}, ${import.meta.env.MODE}`)

if (mode) {
  log(mode === 'deferred' ? 'control: notify after B renders' : 'notify during B render')
  suspendCommit()
  setTimeout(() => {
  b.render(
  h(Flags, {value: {enabled: false}},
  h(Client, {value: 'provided-client'}, h(Notify), h(ReadClient)),
  ),
  )
  setTimeout(() => c.render(h(C)), 100)
  }, 500)
}
```

</details>

## The current behavior

Root B initially reads `undefined` instead of its provided `"provided-client"`. React retries it, and its final DOM can look correct. However, root C subsequently reads **`null` instead of the non-null default `{enabled: true}`**, even though C has no provider:

```text
B client: undefined (provided "provided-client")
B client: "provided-client" (provided "provided-client")
C flags: null (default {"enabled":true})
```

In development, React also reports:

```text
Cannot update a component (`A`) while rendering a different component (`Root`).
Expected host context to exist. This error is likely caused by a bug in React. Please file an issue.
Unexpected pop.
```

The recoverable error callback receives:

```text
There was an error during concurrent rendering but React was able to recover by instead synchronously rendering the entire root.
```

In production, the recoverable error is minified error **#520**, and root C still renders `Root C: null`. This is not just a development warning.

Results in a real headless Chromium browser, for **both** React versions above:

| Trigger | Development | Production |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Synchronous notification, stylesheet commit pending | C reads `null`; B initially loses its provided value | Same |
| Notification deferred with `setTimeout(notify, 0)` | Correct provider/default values; no render errors | Same |
| Synchronous notification, stylesheet omitted | Correct provider/default values; no render errors | Same |

## The expected behavior

C should read `{enabled: true}` and B should read `"provided-client"` under its provider.

The demo deliberately triggers a store notification during rendering to expose the reentrant update. Even if that update should warn or be rejected, React should unwind/recover without corrupting context defaults subsequently read by an unrelated root.

## Suspected path

This looks related to `scheduleUpdateOnFiber` calling `prepareFreshStack` for root A because A has `cancelPendingCommit`, while root B is the root currently rendering. Resetting/unwinding the active stack in that situation appears consistent with the lost provider values and subsequent `Unexpected pop` errors. This is a hypothesis for where to investigate, not a proposed React patch.

Related but different: #37762 concerns a throttled Suspense reveal and passive-effect flushing producing error #177. This reproduction uses a stylesheet-suspended commit and produces incorrect context defaults, without a Suspense boundary or passive-effect store write.

### Description
In `packages/react-client/src/ReactFlightClient.js:4139-4146`, when generating source-mapped fake functions for printed component stack traces received from the server, the client attempts to identify absolute file paths by checking `filename.startsWith('/')`:
```javascript
if (filename.startsWith('/')) {
  // If the filename starts with `/` we assume that it is a file system file
  // rather than relative to the current host. Since on the server fully qualified
  // stack traces use the file path.
  // TODO: What does this look like on Windows?
  filename = 'file://' + filename;
}
```
On Windows hosts, fully qualified absolute file system paths start with a drive letter and colon (e.g. `C:\app\Component.js` or `C:/app/Component.js`), not `/`. As a result, Windows file paths are never normalized into `file://` URLs, leaving source map DevTools group mapping inconsistent across platforms.
React version: 19.3.0 (`main` / commit `16a1d5d`)
## Steps To Reproduce
1. Run an RSC application where the server environment runs on Windows.
2. Render a component that throws or generates a server-side component stack trace where `filename` is an absolute Windows file path (e.g., `C:\repo\App.js`).
3. Inspect the resulting `# sourceURL` comment generated by `ReactFlightClient`.

Link to code example:
`packages/react-client/src/ReactFlightClient.js:4139-4146`:
```javascript
function formatSourceMappedFakeFunction(...) {
  ...
  if (filename.startsWith('/')) {
  // TODO: What does this look like on Windows?
  filename = 'file://' + filename;
  }
  ...
}
```
## The current behavior
On Windows, absolute file paths starting with drive letters (`C:/` or `C:\`) bypass the condition and are not prefixed with `file://`.
## The expected behavior
File paths on Windows (matching drive letter patterns like `/^[a-zA-Z]:[/\\]/`) should be converted to valid `file:///` URLs consistent with POSIX file system paths.

When passing nested `FormData` instances as arguments to a Server Action (`encodeReply` / `decodeReply`), any `FormData` object corresponding to part ID 10 or greater (i.e. `refId >= 10`) has all its entries silently discarded and is decoded as an empty `FormData` (`data.entries().next().done === true`).
This is caused by an integer-radix mismatch between the serializer and deserializer:
1. In `packages/react-client/src/ReactFlightReplyClient.js:707`, field entries are keyed with a decimal prefix:
  ```javascript
  const prefix = formFieldPrefix + '_' + refId + '_';
  ```
  When `refId` is `10`, the entries are appended as `formFieldPrefix + '_10_' + key`.
2. The reference token emitted by `serializeFormDataReference` (`packages/react-client/src/ReactFlightReplyClient.js:138`) is formatted as hexadecimal:
  ```javascript
  function serializeFormDataReference(id: number): string {
  return '$K' + id.toString(16);
  }
  ```
  When `refId` is `10`, it emits `'$Ka'`.
3. In `packages/react-server/src/ReactFlightReplyServer.js:1765-1773`, the server slices off `'$K'` and reconstructs the prefix directly from the hex string without calling `parseInt(..., 16)`:
  ```javascript
  case 'K': {
  const stringId = value.slice(2);
  const formPrefix = response._prefix + '_' + stringId + '_';
  return consumeBackingEntries(response._formData, formPrefix);
  }
  ```
  The server looks for `response._prefix + '_a_'`.
4. In `consumeBackingEntries` (`packages/react-server/src/ReactFlightReplyBackingFormData.js:130-141`), the key `_10_...` fails `key.startsWith(formPrefix)` (`_a_`). The loop immediately breaks, returning an empty `FormData` and leaving `keyPointer` at `0`, causing subsequent `FormData` objects in the payload to also fail and return empty.
Every other handler in `ReactFlightReplyServer.js` (`case 'B'`, `case 'Q'`, `case 'W'`, `case 'A'`, `case 'R'`, etc.) parses the hex reference ID to integer via `parseInt(..., 16)` before constructing the key. `case 'K'` is the only handler that omitted this step.
React version: 19.3.0 (`main` / commit `16a1d5d`)

React version:

## Steps To Reproduce

1. Call a Server Action with a payload containing 10 or more `FormData` objects (or a payload with 9 other objects like Maps/Sets/Blobs followed by a `FormData`).
2. Inside the server action handler, inspect `Array.from(formData.entries())` or call `formData.get('someKey')` on the 10th `FormData`.
3. Observe that items 0 to 8 retain their entries, but item 9 (`refId = 10`) is completely empty and returns `null` for all keys.
Link to code example:
A minimal reproduction test case added to `packages/react-server-dom-webpack/src/__tests__/ReactFlightDOMReply-test.js`:
```javascript
it('can pass the 10th or later FormData in a reply payload', async () => {
  const list = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < 12; i++) {
  const fd = new FormData();
  fd.set('key_' + i, 'value_' + i);
