When the callback passed to `act` throws, synchronously or by rejecting, `act` rethrows the error but leaves `ReactSharedInternals.actQueue` pointing at its queue. Every later update made outside `act` is pushed to that queue, which nothing flushes, so it never renders. The next successful `act` call flushes it.

This hits tests that assert on an expected error and then wait for an update outside `act`, for example with Testing Library's `waitFor`, which leaves the act environment while it polls. Those tests time out.

