## The current behavior

In development React logs:

> There are two `<ViewTransition name="cover-1">` components with the same name mounted at the same time. This is not supported and will cause View Transitions to error.

but only one element has `view-transition-name: cover-1` in the DOM at that point, so the warning is a false positive.

## The expected behavior

No warning, because the first fiber no longer has that name.

## Cause (as far as I can tell from `main`)

`packages/react-reconciler/src/ReactFiberDuplicateViewTransitions.js` keeps a module-level `mountedNamedViewTransitions` map keyed by name. `trackNamedViewTransition` writes `name -> fiber` on layout commit. `untrackNamedViewTransition` is only called from `ReactFiberCommitWork.js` on deletion and when an Offscreen boundary hides, and it reads the **current** `memoizedProps.name`.

So when `name` changes from `'cover-1'` to `undefined` (or to `'cover-2'`) on a fiber that stays mounted:
- nothing removes `'cover-1'` from the map at update time, and
- when the fiber is deleted later, `untrack` reads `name === undefined` (or `'cover-2'`) and never deletes the `'cover-1'` entry.

The stale `'cover-1'` entry then makes any other fiber that mounts with that name trigger the warning (once per name, via `didWarnAboutName`).

This is DEV only, there is no production impact and the transition itself works. It is just noisy in apps that keep a stable `ViewTransition` wrapper and toggle its name, for example shared-element cards where only the active one is named.

A possible fix would be to untrack the previous name when `name` changes during update, but I would like to hear whether that is the intended direction before sending a PR.

Related: #35983, #36728 (name changes and updates, a different symptom).

## Summary

BuildHIR lowers every `switch` case test before the `switch` terminal, last case first. A case test that calls a function can therefore run eagerly and out of order, instead of lazily in source order. `lowerReorderableExpression` is meant to admit only case tests where that can't be observed, and it already rejects e.g. `case props.kind:`. But `isReorderableExpression` accepts calls and `new` whose callee and arguments are reorderable. That allowance exists for destructuring defaults (#36107), where the default value is still lowered lazily inside a ternary. Switch case tests share the helper, so a later case test can run even when an earlier case matches:

```js
function Component({value}) {
  const labels = [];
  switch (value) {
  case null:
  labels.push('empty');
  break;
  case lower(value):
  labels.push('lowercase');
  break;
  case upper(value):
  labels.push('uppercase');
  break;
  }
  return labels;
}
```

compiles to

```js
  if ($[0] !== value) {
  labels = [];

  const t1 = upper(value);
  const t2 = lower(value);
  bb0: switch (value) {
  case null: {
  labels.push("empty");
  break bb0;
  }
  case t2: {
```

With `value: null` the original returns `["empty"]`. The compiled component calls `upper(null)` before entering the switch and throws during render. Whether the tests get hoisted depends on later passes. Here the switch is inside the reactive scope for `labels`; when nothing forces that, codegen happens to inline the tests back into their cases.

This adds an `allowCalls` flag to `lowerReorderableExpression`/`isReorderableExpression`. Switch case tests pass `false`, so a call or `new` anywhere in a case test now reports the existing "cannot be safely reordered" Todo and the function is left uncompiled. Destructuring defaults pass `true` and behave as before. The same change is made in the Rust port (`build_hir.rs`).

## How did you test this change?

Added two fixtures, following the pattern used for the implicit `arguments` bailout:

- `error.todo-switch-case-test-call`: the Todo error.
- `switch-case-test-call-bailout-preserves-renders` (`@panicThreshold:"none"`): sprout renders the original and the compiler output for `null`, `'a'` and `'A'` and compares them.

With the fix reverted, both fail on `yarn snap` and `yarn snap --rust`:

```
