## Summary

A `for..of` or `for..in` loop that assigns to a variable declared before the loop hits an invariant in codegen instead of a Todo bailout:

```js
function Component(props) {
  let item = null;
  for (item of props.items) {
  if (item.selected) break;
  }
  return <div>{item?.name}</div>;
}
```

```
Invariant: Unexpected Reassign variable in for..of collection
```

`for (key in obj)` gives `Invariant: Destructure should never be Reassign as it would be an Object/ArrayPattern`. The Rust port fails the same way (`Unexpected Reassign variable in for..of collection` / `in for..in collection`).

#31710 started lowering these heads as a `Reassign` store, but codegen was never updated for them. Updating codegen alone isn't safe. The store sits in the loop's test block, so the HIR says it also runs on the last check, when the iterator is done. In JS the variable keeps its value at that point, so the compiler treats the value from before the loop as dead. When I tried emitting `for (item of ...)` in codegen, the example above compiled `let item = null;` to `let item;`, and this fixture failed snap's eval check:

```js
function Component(props) {
  let item = 'none';
  for (item of props.items) {
  }
  return [item];
}
// params: [{items: []}]
```

Without the compiler it returns `['none']`. Compiled, the initial value is gone.

So this records a Todo for non-declaration heads (`Identifier`, patterns, member expressions) before anything is lowered, like the for-await bailout next to it. It also removes the now-unreachable `Reassign` lowering. The Rust port's `build_hir.rs` gets the same change.

