## Summary

`validateNoFreezingKnownMutableFunctions` rejects passing a function with *known* mutations (e.g. a callback that closes over and mutates a local `Map`) where a frozen value is expected. It only recorded that fact for `FunctionExpression` values, and only propagated it through `LoadLocal`/`StoreLocal`, so the check is bypassed whenever the same function is stored in an object literal and read back through a property — the common shape for event handler objects:

```js
function useFoo() {
  const cache = new Map();
  const handlers = {
  onClick() {
  cache.set('key', 'value');
  },
  };
  useHook(handlers.onClick); // no error today
}
```

while the `useHook(() => { cache.set(...) })` and `const onClick = () => {...}; useHook(onClick)` variants both report "Cannot modify local variables after render completes".

This PR:

- handles `ObjectMethod` the same as `FunctionExpression` (the mutation analysis is factored into `recordKnownMutability`);
- records the statically-known property values of each `ObjectExpression` and follows `obj.prop` reads and `const {prop} = obj` destructuring, so a read resolves to the function assigned to that property;
- follows aliases through `LoadLocal`/`StoreLocal` for the object-property map as well, so `const h = handlers; useHook(h.onClick)` works;
- only follows statically-known property names, so a property that does not hold a known-mutable function is unaffected (see the `allow-object-method-sibling-argument` fixture);
- mirrors all of the above in the Rust port (`react_compiler_validation`).

## How did you test this change?

- Three new fixtures:
  - `error.invalid-object-method-argument-mutates-local-variable` (shorthand method read through a property) — fails without this change.
  - `error.invalid-destructured-function-argument-mutates-local-variable` (`const {onClick} = handlers`) — fails without this change.
  - `allow-object-method-sibling-argument` (only a *sibling* method mutates the local) — still compiles, guarding against over-reporting.
  Both error fixtures report the same diagnostic and the same "This modifies `cache`" location as the existing `error.invalid-hook-function-argument-mutates-local-variable`.
- Ran the full fixture suite (1839 fixtures, both before and after the change): the changed/failed list is identical to `main` (the 25 pre-existing `changed` and 3 pre-existing `failed` entries are unrelated fbt/debugger fixtures), so no existing snapshot changes besides the three new fixtures.
- `tsc --noEmit`, `eslint` (babel-plugin-react-compiler) and `prettier` pass.

## Notes

- This is a diagnostics-coverage change: code that previously compiled now reports an error, but only when the known-mutating function is statically reachable through an object literal. `validateNoFreezingKnownMutableFunctions` is off by default in the compiler and enabled by `eslint-plugin-react-compiler`.
- The Rust port is updated to match TS; I could not run `cargo check` locally (no Rust toolchain on this machine), so CI covers it.

## Summary

Some `CompilerError.invariant` calls build their `reason` or `description` with a print helper (`printInstruction`, `printInstructionValue`, `printPlace`, `printAliasingEffect`, ...). The options object is evaluated before `invariant` checks the condition, so those strings are built on every call, even though they are only used when the invariant fails.

A few of these sites are hot:

- `markInstructionIds` prints every instruction each time it runs, and it runs several times per function. For a `FunctionExpression` that prints the whole nested function.
- `InferenceState.define` prints the instruction value on every call.
- `InferenceState.kind` and `values` print the place on every lookup.
- `applyEffect` prints the effect for every `Create`, `CreateFrom`, `CreateFunction` and `Assign`.

This wraps the 28 invariants whose message calls a print helper in an `if` on the negated condition, so the message is only built when the invariant fails. The thrown errors and their messages are unchanged.

In a CPU profile of compiling the DevTools views (see below) on main, these print calls were about 4% of samples, plus the garbage they create.

