## Summary

`constantPropagation` treats every `LoadGlobal` as a compile-time constant, but `LoadGlobal` also represents bindings declared in the same module (`NonLocalBinding.kind === 'ModuleLocal'`, e.g. a module-level `let`). Since the pass forwards the *value* of a load to its use site, a forwarded load observes the value at the **use site** rather than at the **original load**.

Fixes #37888.

## Repro

```js
let pending = 'value';

function Component() {
  const handleClick = () => {
  const copy = pending;
  pending = null;
  console.log(copy);
  };
  return <button onClick={handleClick}>Click</button>;
}
```

On `main`, the handler is compiled to:

```js
function _temp() {
  pending = null;
  console.log(pending); // logs null
}
```

`const copy = pending` is dropped and every later use reads `pending` after the assignment. The new fixture (`constant-propagation-module-scope-copy`) fails on `main` and passes with this change:

```js
function _temp() {
  const copy = pending;
  pending = null;
  console.log(copy); // logs 'value'
}
```

Note this is not only about the literal store: any call between the load and the use can mutate a module binding, so the load cannot be moved past it either. Scanning the function for `StoreGlobal` would not be sufficient.

## Fix

`ModuleLocal` bindings are no longer treated as constants. Import bindings cannot be reassigned and keep propagating, so `constant-propagate-global-phis-constant` and the `LoadGlobal`-based validation fixtures (`error.capitalized-function-call-aliased`, `error.invalid-assign-hook-to-local`) are unaffected.

I also updated `docs/passes/04-constantPropagation.md`, which documented `LoadGlobal` as "directly constant".

## Trade-off

`NonLocalBinding` doesn't record whether a module binding is declared with `const`, so module-level `const`s lose this optimization as well. That is why `object-expression-computed-key-non-reactive` changes: `const key = SCALE` (with `SCALE` a module-level `const`) is now kept instead of inlined into `{ [SCALE]: t0 }`. The output is still correct, just slightly less compact. Recording constness on `NonLocalBinding` would recover it, but that touches the HIR type shared with the Rust port, so I left it out of this fix.

