## The bug: React Compiler's SSR mode removes useState setter without checking to see if the setter is used in a SSR pass

With `outputMode: 'ssr'`, the React Compiler turns `const [count, setCount] = useState(0)` into `const count = 0`  inlining the state. That deletes `setCount`, but the pass never checked whether anything else used it. So this:

```js
const [open, setOpen] = useState(false);
return <Dialog open={open} onOpenChange={setOpen} />;
```

became `const open = false; return <Dialog open={open} onOpenChange={setOpen} />;`, which crashes with `setOpen is not defined`. The same happened when the setter went into a context value, was returned from a custom hook, or was called during render.

This issue was reproduced in both the Rust and TypeScript implementations, though the TypeScript implementation escaped some cases but not all.

## The fix: only remove useState setters that aren't used later in SSR pass

The compiler now deletes a setter only if nothing left in the server copy uses it.

The compiler already removes code that never runs on the server: event handlers, effects, and the `on…` and `ref` props on HTML tags like `<button>`. It now makes those removals first, then works out what the compiler's existing cleanup step (dead code elimination) would delete, without deleting anything yet, and uses that to check each piece of state it wants to inline. Now there are additional checks to determine when/if to inline state:

- **If the setter was removed, inline state (see exception in the next section)**: if nothing left uses a `useState`'s setter the state is inlined as before . For example, a setter used only by a button's `onClick` handler goes away with the handler, so `const [count, setCount] = useState(0)` still becomes `const count = 0`.
- **If the setter is still there, don't inline state**: code that runs on the server reads it, so the `useState` call stays. The `Dialog` example above now renders instead of crashing.

## Multiple renders in SSR and updated values passed to useState

If values passed to useState could change we also *should not* inline state. If a setter or a custom hook might change some state during render, React renders the component again and state passed to useState could change.

`useState(start)` saves a value: React stores `start` on the first render, and later renders of the component get the saved value back, ignoring `start`. Inlined state saves nothing, so `start` is recomputed on every render. The two match as long as the component renders only once.

On the server a component usually renders once. But when it calls one of its own setters while rendering, React throws that render away and renders the component again with the new state, [on the server too](<url>). Inlined state can then come out different from the saved value:

```js
function Pager({requestedPage, pageCount}) {
  const [page, setPage] = useState(requestedPage);
  const [wasOutOfRange] = useState(page > pageCount);
  if (page > pageCount) {
  setPage(pageCount); // forces a second render
  }
  return (
  <p>
  Page {page} of {pageCount}
  {wasOutOfRange && ' (page not found, showing the last page)'}
  </p>
  );
}
```

`wasOutOfRange` has no setter, so checking setters alone would inline it as `const wasOutOfRange = page > pageCount`. If `requestedPage` is `7` and `pageCount` is `5`, the second render recomputes it as `5 > 5`, so the server renders `Page 5 of 5` without the `(page not found, showing the last page)` notice. The browser renders the notice, which leads to a hydration mismatch.

The compiler can't tell whether a setter will actually run during render. So if a setter is still used, or the component calls a custom hook (which can call its own setters during render, or return a setter the component calls), it keeps every `useState` whose value is used, unless it starts at a literal like `0` or `'ready'`. A `useReducer` with an `init` function keeps its hook even if its value is never used, because React calls `init` only on the first render. Other state whose value is never used is still inlined.

## Summary

`serializeIONode` picks the name of an I/O entry from the name of the first first-party function in the stack. That name can include the type name that the function was called on. Since that context is usually not useful, the name is reduced to just the function, e.g. `Window.fetch` becomes `fetch`.

The `<anonymous>.` case copied the slice length straight from the `Window.` case above it, but that prefix is **12 characters long, not 7**. So instead of stripping the prefix it damaged the name:

```
'<anonymous>.'.length  === 12
'<anonymous>.fetch'.slice(7)  === 'ous>.fetch'  // current, broken
'<anonymous>.fetch'.slice(12) === 'fetch'  // intended
```

This was introduced in #33392 and is the only place in the repo that strips this prefix.

### Reproducing

V8 derives the type name of a method call from the receiver, and `Symbol.toStringTag` is honored there. An object that reports itself as `<anonymous>` therefore produces stack frames named `<anonymous>.getData`:

```js
const client = {
  [Symbol.toStringTag]: '<anonymous>',
  getData(text) {
  return new Promise(resolve => {
  setTimeout(() => resolve(text.toUpperCase()), 1);
  });
  },
};
```

`CallSite.prototype.toString()` for that frame is `<anonymous>.getData (file:1:1)`, so this shape is produced by both the structured stack parser and the string stack parser.

Starting I/O from that method, the recorded stack frame is correctly named `<anonymous>.getData`, but the name reported for the I/O itself — which is what DevTools shows — is mangled:

```json
{
  "awaited": {
  "name": "ous>.getData",
  "stack": [["<anonymous>.getData", ".../ReactFlightAsyncDebugInfo-test.js", 563, 16, 562, 16]]
  }
}
```

After the fix the reported name is `getData`.

## How did you test this change?

Added a regression test to `ReactFlightAsyncDebugInfo-test.js` which starts I/O from a method of an object whose `Symbol.toStringTag` is `<anonymous>`. It fails before the fix with `Expected: "getData"` / `Received: "ous>.getData"`, and the test also asserts the recorded stack frame is still `<anonymous>.getData` so it fails loudly if V8 ever stops producing that shape. The test is appended at the end of the suite so that the line numbers referenced by the existing inline snapshots do not shift.

- `yarn test ReactFlightAsyncDebugInfo --runInBand` — 21 passed
- `yarn test ReactFlight --runInBand` — 437 passed; the 3 `ReactFlightDOMEdge` `SharedArrayBuffer` failures are pre-existing on `main` (verified by stashing the fix and running the same suite)
- `yarn linc`
- `node scripts/prettier/index.js check-changed`

## Summary

`flushSyncWorkAcrossRoots_impl` sets the module-level `isFlushingWork` reentrancy guard before looping over the scheduled roots and resets it after the loop, but not in a `finally`:

```js
isFlushingWork = true;
do {
  // ...
  performSyncWorkOnRoot(root, nextLanes);
  // ...
} while (didPerformSomeWork);
isFlushingWork = false;
```

If `performSyncWorkOnRoot` throws, the guard stays `true` for the lifetime of the page. Every later call returns early at the `if (isFlushingWork) return;` check, so sync updates are never flushed again. Clicks still dispatch to React handlers and `setState` still marks lanes as pending (`root.pendingLanes` keeps the `SyncLane` bit, `root.callbackNode` is `null`), but nothing ever renders. The app looks completely frozen until the page is reloaded.

Most errors are caught inside the render and commit phases, but some host config calls are not. One example is `prepareForCommit` in react-dom, which reads the selection of the focused element (`selectionStart` or `window.getSelection()`) before mutating the DOM. If that throws (we hit this in production with a rich text editor and an embedder that patches selection APIs), the error escapes `performSyncWorkOnRoot`.

It's worse when the flush was triggered by `flushSync` inside a `try/catch` or a promise chain. The error is swallowed by user code, there is no "Uncaught" error in the console, and the only symptom is that the UI stops responding.

This PR wraps the loop in `try/finally`. The error still propagates to the caller exactly as before, but the guard is reset so subsequent sync work is flushed.

Earlier versions of this loop (e.g. at the time of #26763) wrapped each `performSyncWorkOnRoot` call in a `try/catch` that collected errors and rethrew them after resetting the flag, so this case used to be handled. That protection was removed in a later refactor.

The diff is mostly re-indentation; it's easier to review with whitespace changes hidden (`?w=1`).

### Minimal repro

```js
import {flushSync} from 'react-dom';
import {createRoot} from 'react-dom/client';

const container = document.getElementById('root');
const root = createRoot(container);

function App({text}) {
  return (
  <>
  <input />
  <span>{text}</span>
  </>
  );
}

flushSync(() => root.render(<App text="A" />));

const input = container.querySelector('input');
input.focus();
let shouldThrow = true;
Object.defineProperty(input, 'selectionStart', {
  configurable: true,
  get() {
  if (shouldThrow) {
  shouldThrow = false;
  throw new Error('Selection read failed');
  }
  return 0;
  },
});

try {
  flushSync(() => root.render(<App text="B" />));
} catch (e) {
  // Caught by the caller, as library code commonly does.
}

flushSync(() => root.render(<App text="C" />));
console.log(container.textContent); // Before: "A" (and all later updates are dropped). After: "C".
```

## How did you test this change?

Added `packages/react-dom/src/__tests__/ReactDOMFlushSyncCommitError-test.js`. It focuses an `<input>`, makes the commit-phase `selectionStart` read throw once inside `flushSync`, then asserts that the next `flushSync` update is rendered.

- Without the fix the test fails (`Expected: "C"`, `Received: "A"`).
- With the fix it passes:
  - `yarn test packages/react-dom/src/__tests__/ReactDOMFlushSyncCommitError-test.js`
  - `yarn test -r=stable packages/react-dom/src/__tests__/ReactDOMFlushSyncCommitError-test.js`
  - `yarn test --prod packages/react-dom/src/__tests__/ReactDOMFlushSyncCommitError-test.js`
- `yarn test packages/react-reconciler` plus `ReactDOMRoot`, `ReactDOMFiber` and `ReactDOMSelect`: no new failures. `ReactUse-test` and `ReactMemo-test` fail with the same 4 tests on unmodified `main` in my Windows environment.
- `yarn linc` and `prettier` pass. I couldn't run `yarn flow` locally (it fails to spawn on Windows), so I'm relying on CI for Flow; the change doesn't touch any types.

