After running ASan on one of my project that was unpredictably crashing, I found a OOB read due to a vectorisation. I'm providing a few small cases that reproduce this bug below: `smallvec.rs`, `default.rs`, and `crash.rs`. The smallvec version resembles the code closest to my original (minus the outline and `black_box`). The default version reproduces the OOB with no dependencies. The `crash.rs` demonstrates a crash when guard pages are used.

In my testing, the dependency free version triggers a 3-byte OOB read when executed on an AMD Ryzen 9 7900 with either the default `x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu` target or any relevant `-Ctarget-feature` combination of `"+sse3"`, `"+sse4.1"`, `"+sse4.2"`, `"+avx"`, `"+avx2"`, `"+avx512f"`, `"+avx512{cd,bw,dq,vl}"`, or `-Ctarget-cpu=x86-64v{2,3,4}`.

Though I only tested on 1.99.1, @cuviper was able to reproduce back to *at least* 1.66 (at the point of `black_box` being stabilised); currently, not tested further back. We were not able to find evidence of information disclosure, as the OOB reads are discarded.

As pointed out by @cuviper, <url> looks relevant, and indeed follows a very similar setup.

