### Unix vs Windows diff 


|  | Unix | Windows |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Module | `std::os::unix::net` | `std::os::windows::net` |
| Types | `UnixStream`, `UnixListener`, `SocketAddr`, `Incoming` | same names |
| Inner fd access | `AsFd` / `AsRawFd` (`OwnedFd`, `RawFd`) | `AsSocket` / `AsRawSocket` (`OwnedSocket`, `RawSocket`) |
| `UnixDatagram` | yes | Not supported on the platform |
| `SocketAddr` from abstract namespace | yes | Not supported on the platform |
| Ancillary / `SCM_RIGHTS` fd passing | yes | Not supported on the platform |
| `pair()` | yes | Not supported on the platform |


The core APIs for UDS (UnixStream, UnixListener, etc) are largely the same across Unix and Windows when using socket paths.  

Windows does not [support abstract paths in AF_UNIX](<url>), and as such UDS  on Windows would not support creating a SocketAddr from an abstract path. 

Due to this divergence, I recommend  that we maintain the current structure with two separate implementations in OS specific locations (std::os::unix::net and std::os::windows::net).  



Out of scope for this stabilization is presenting a unified interface if one exists. 

