I've been sitting on this for a while, and really just want to know if this is the right direction. This idea was discussed originally in [#t-compiler > &#96;become&#96; failing compilation inside &#96;cfg(feature = "...")&#96;](<url>).

In summary, there are targets (s390x, powerpc elfv1, current wasm baseline) that fundamentally cannot implement guaranteed tail calls. There are more backends for which LLVM in practice does not (correctly) implement guaranteed tail calls. The idea is to have a fallback mechanism for those targets that at least makes (mutually) recursive functions not blow the stack.

This is unsatisfying in some important ways: guaranteed tail calls are (beyond functional programming homework exercises) a tool for optimization. This fallback mechanism cannot handle indirect (via a function pointer) calls, while that is the main way that tail calls are used in real-world projects. So, the idea here is that the basic uses of `become` do just work but practically the value of this mechanism is somewhat debatable. Nevertheless, T-lang expressed that they really want a fallback mechanism.

The idea here is that a function that uses `become` to tail call another function is transformed (via our shim mechanism) into a function that returns a continuation: a piece of data describing the call to make next. 

For example, given these mutually recursive functions:

```rust
fn is_even(n: u32) -> bool { 
  match n {
  0 => true,
  _ => become is_odd(n - 1),
  }
}

fn is_odd(n: u32) -> bool { 
  match n {
  0 => false,
  _ => become is_even(n - 1),
  }
}
```

we derive these shims:

```rust
#[lang = "tail_next"]
pub enum TailNext<Args, Ret> {
  /// The call chain has bottomed out.
  #[lang = "tail_next_done"]
  Done(Ret),
  /// The call chain continues.
  #[lang = "tail_next_call"]
  Call(fn(Args) -> TailNext<Args, Ret>, Args),
}

fn is_even_shim(args: (u32,)) -> TailNext<(u32,), bool> { 
  let (n,) = args;
  match n {
  0 => TailNext::Done(true),
  _ => TailNext::Call(is_odd_shim, (n - 1,)),
  }
}

fn is_odd_shim(n: (u32,)) -> TailNext<(u32,), bool> { 
  let (n,) = args;
  match n {
  0 => TailNext::Done(false),
  _ => TailNext::Call(is_even_shim, (n - 1,)),
  }
}
```

And convert the original body into a call to the `tail_eval` lang item:

```rust
#[lang = "tail_eval"]
pub fn tail_eval<Args, Ret>(mut f: fn(Args) -> TailNext<Args, Ret>, mut args: Args) -> Ret {
  loop {
  match f(args) {
  TailNext::Call(next_f, next_args) => {
  f = next_f;
  args = next_args;
  }
  TailNext::Done(ret) => return ret,
  }
  }
}

fn is_even(n: u32) -> bool { 
  tail_eval(is_even_shim, (n,))
}

fn is_odd(n: u32) -> bool { 
  tail_eval(is_odd_shim, (n,))
}
```

Creating the shim for `is_even` requires that we know what shim is generated for `is_odd`, hence the limitation that this transformation does not work for indirect calls: we have no way of finding the shim in that case.

---

So, that's the idea. My execution of it probably violates a bunch of MIR assumptions at the moment. This branch needs more testing, etc. But before I spend the time on that, I'd like to validate that this looks roughly right.

Currently the fallback can only be triggered by passing `-Zforce-tail-call-fallback`, I didn't yet want to change behavior by default. A lot of the changes are just adding `ShimKind::TailCall` in the right places, and then the logic needed to build the shim (verbose but relatively straightforward).

One part I'm less confident in is the query stuff, and whether that is robust across crates. We need to keep the body of a function if a downstream crate may want to generate a shim from it.

