### Version and scope

Reproduced with published `deno_core = 0.413.0`, V8 150.4.0, on Windows x64 in an embedder using only public APIs. The same fast-path condition is present in the current `libs/core/modules/map/dynamic.rs`.

### Expected / actual

After `JsRuntime::mod_evaluate()` reaches a pending top-level await, a subsequent `import()` of that module (or an async descendant) must remain pending until evaluation settles. Currently the import can resolve immediately with a namespace whose exports are still in TDZ. This can break asynchronous game/application initialization.

### Minimal embedding reproduction

With `deno_core = "=0.413.0"` and Tokio's current-thread runtime:

```rust
use std::{rc::Rc, task::{Context, Waker}};
use deno_core::{JsRuntime, RuntimeOptions, StaticModuleLoader, ModuleSpecifier, PollEventLoopOptions};

fn main() {
  let reactor = tokio::runtime::Builder::new_current_thread().enable_all().build().unwrap();
  let _entered = reactor.enter();
  let url = ModuleSpecifier::parse("<url>").unwrap();
  let loader = StaticModuleLoader::with(url.clone(),
  "globalThis.entered=true; await new Promise(r=>globalThis.release=r); globalThis.completed=true;");
  let mut js = JsRuntime::new(RuntimeOptions {
  module_loader: Some(Rc::new(loader)), ..Default::default()
  });
  // The embedder can resolve the await from its own event sources.
  js.op_state().borrow().external_ops_tracker.ref_op();
  let id = reactor.block_on(js.load_main_es_module(&url)).unwrap();
  let _evaluation = js.mod_evaluate(id);
  js.execute_script("<url>",
  "globalThis.settled='pending'; import('<url>').then(()=>settled='resolved');").unwrap();
  let mut cx = Context::from_waker(Waker::noop());
  for _ in 0..4 { let _ = js.poll_event_loop(&mut cx, PollEventLoopOptions::default()); }
  let result = js.execute_script("<url>",
  "JSON.stringify([globalThis.entered,globalThis.completed,globalThis.settled])").unwrap();
  deno_core::scope!(scope, js);
  let result = deno_core::v8::Local::new(scope, result);
  println!("{}", result.to_rust_string_lossy(scope));
}
```

Actual: `[true,null,"resolved"]`. Expected before calling `release()`: `[true,null,"pending"]`.

### Investigation and proposed fix

`ModuleStatus::Evaluated` does not mean the asynchronous evaluation promise has fulfilled. The shortcut checks `pending_dyn_mod_evaluations`, which does not cover a statically initiated evaluation and can miss an async descendant when only the importing root is tracked.

I am preparing a small PR that excludes async graphs from this shortcut and lets the existing evaluation-promise path handle them. In an isolated copy of the published crate, the change passes an embedding matrix covering roots/descendants, fulfillment/rejection, exception identity, cached namespace identity, and single fetch/evaluation (normal and JITless). No CLI behavior or full monorepo test result is claimed here.

AI assistance: this report, reproducer, and proposed patch were prepared with OpenAI Codex; the native checks described above were executed.

Version: Deno 2.9.8 (x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu)

In streaming mode, `TextDecoder` drops a U+FEFF that starts a later `decode()` call when the stream began with other text. In the [serialize I/O queue](<url>) algorithm, the decoder sets "BOM seen" on the first item, whatever it is, so only a U+FEFF at the very start of the stream is a byte order mark.

```js
const d = new TextDecoder();
const out =
  d.decode(new Uint8Array([0x61]), {stream: true}) +
  d.decode(new Uint8Array([0xef, 0xbb, 0xbf, 0x62]), {stream: true}) +
  d.decode();
console.log([...out].map(c => c.codePointAt(0).toString(16)));
```

Expected: `["61", "feff", "62"]` &mdash; what Node 26.12.1 and Bun 1.4.4 print.

Actual: `["61", "62"]`.

When the stream itself starts with a BOM, Deno drops it and keeps a later U+FEFF, so "BOM seen" appears to be set only when a BOM is actually removed. The output then depends on where the input happens to split into chunks.

Version: Deno 2.9.8 (also reproduced on 2.9.6 and 2.9.7; x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu)

## Summary

When a `fetch` response body arrives as a chunked stream (no `content-length`) in small HTTP
chunks, each `reader.read()` returns a `Uint8Array` that is a view over its **own fresh 64 KiB
(65,536-byte) `ArrayBuffer`**, whatever the chunk's `value.byteLength`. A consumer that holds the
chunks until the body ends (the common "read everything, then concatenate" pattern) therefore
pins **reads × 64 KiB**, not the body size.

Is this intended? If it is, it would help to document it, because nothing in the Streams API
suggests that keeping a 1 KB chunk keeps 64 KiB alive. If it isn't, returning exact-size
buffers (or slices of a shared pool that are freed with their chunks) would fix it.

## Reproduction (self-contained)

```ts
// deno run -A repro.ts <chunkBytes> <sendContentLength: 0|1>
const CH = Number(Deno.args[0] ?? 1000);
const withLen = Deno.args[1] === "1";
const body = new Uint8Array(4 * 1024 * 1024).fill(0x61);

const server = Deno.serve({ port: 0, onListen: () => {} }, () => {
  let off = 0;
  const stream = new ReadableStream<Uint8Array>({
  pull(c) {
  if (off >= body.length) return c.close();
  c.enqueue(body.subarray(off, off + CH));
  off += CH;
  },
  });
  const headers: Record<string, string> = {};
  if (withLen) headers["content-length"] = String(body.length);
  return new Response(stream, { headers });
});

const res = await fetch(`<url>}/`);
const reader = res.body!.getReader();
const held: Uint8Array[] = [];
const bufs = new Set<ArrayBufferLike>();
while (true) {
  const { done, value } = await reader.read();
  if (done) break;
  bufs.add(value.buffer);
  held.push(value);
}
let backing = 0;
for (const b of bufs) backing += b.byteLength;
console.log({ reads: held.length, distinctBuffers: bufs.size, backingMiB: backing / 2 ** 20 });
await server.shutdown();
```

Results for the same 4 MiB body:

| Delivery | Reads | Distinct buffers | Backing |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 1 KB chunks, no `content-length` (`repro.ts 1000 0`) | 4,209 | 4,209 | **263 MiB** |
| 1 KB chunks, with `content-length` (`repro.ts 1000 1`) | 71 | 71 | 4.4 MiB |
| 64 KiB chunks, no `content-length` (`repro.ts 65536 0`) | 78 | 78 | 4.9 MiB |

Every buffer in that column measures exactly 65,536 bytes, so the backing total is just
reads × 64 KiB. The amplification only shows up with chunked bodies sent in sub-64 KiB pieces:
with a `content-length` or large chunks, reads are coalesced to about 64 KiB and little is
wasted, so testing with either of those won't reproduce it.

## Where it comes from

The response body is a resource-backed byte stream. `createResponseBodyStream()` calls
`readableStreamForRid(responseBodyRid)` (`ext/fetch/26_fetch.js`, line 367 at v2.9.8), and that helper
builds a `type: "bytes"` stream with `autoAllocateChunkSize: DEFAULT_CHUNK_SIZE`, where
`DEFAULT_CHUNK_SIZE = 64 * 1024` (`ext/web/06_streams.js`, lines 1159 and 1275 at v2.9.8). Its `pull()`
reads into the auto-allocated view (`core.read(rid, controller.byobRequest.view)`) and then
`respond(bytesRead)`s. A chunk smaller than the auto-allocated view is what leaves the rest of
that 64 KiB buffer alive for as long as the returned view is held.

This is not fetch-specific — `readableStreamForRid` is the generic rid-backed stream path
(`Deno.FsFile.readable`, `Deno.stdin`, sockets, …). A 5-byte file read pins 64 KiB with no
server involved:

```ts
const path = await Deno.makeTempFile();
await Deno.writeTextFile(path, "hello"); // 5 bytes
const file = await Deno.open(path);
const { value } = await file.readable.getReader().read();
console.log(value.byteLength, value.buffer.byteLength); // 5 65536
```

(Measured on 2.9.7 and 2.9.8: 100-byte file → `chunkBytes: [100]`, one distinct buffer,
`backingBytes: 65536`.)

## Impact

We hit this in production through Supabase Edge Runtime, which inherits the behaviour (it
depends on the upstream `deno_web` crate — `deno_web 0.213.0` today — so a fix here reaches it
on a dependency bump): a 256 MB isolate receiving a 14 MB embeddings response in ~1 KB chunks
was repeatedly killed for exceeding its memory limit. Replaying that response on standalone
Deno, the held chunks pinned ~900 MB of backing buffers. The consumer that held the chunks was
the AI SDK's `readResponseWithSizeLimit`; that side was filed as vercel/ai#23397 and has since been fixed there. Consumers can
work around it by copying each chunk as it arrives, but they have no reason to suspect they
need to.

