Also seen on 2.9.6. Linux x86_64, plain HTTP/1.1 over TCP (the Deno-owned raw HTTP/1.1 serve path).

## Summary

When a handler responds before consuming the request body, `Deno.serve` always reads the rest of the body to the end before it reuses or closes the connection. There is no cap, so an early 413 on a 64 MiB upload still costs the server the full 64 MiB. For an untouched body Deno already decides to close the connection (`connection: close`), yet it still reads the whole body before closing.

## Repro

`unread-body.ts` and its helper `raw-client.ts` are at the end of this issue; run `deno run --allow-net --allow-env --allow-read unread-body.ts`. The handler answers a 64 MiB upload with a 413 after reading one chunk (or nothing). A raw HTTP/1.1 client over `node:net` keeps sending the body after the response and counts how much the server accepts within 5 s.

```ts
const handler = async (req: Request): Promise<Response> => {
  const path = new URL(req.url).pathname
  if (path === "/health") return new Response("ok")
  if (path.startsWith("/partial")) {
  const reader = req.body!.getReader()
  await reader.read() // read one chunk, leave the rest
  if (path.endsWith("/cancel")) await reader.cancel()
  else reader.releaseLock()
  }
  const headers = new URL(req.url).searchParams.has("close") ? { connection: "close" } : {}
  return new Response("too large", { status: 413, headers })
}
```

Output on 2.9.7 (every case was also run with `Transfer-Encoding: chunked`, same result):

```
== /untouched (content-length)
response: HTTP/1.1 413 Payload Too Large | connection: ["close"]
rest of body: 63.06 MiB of 64.00 MiB accepted in 103 ms, then complete
next request on the same connection: error (ECONNRESET)

== /partial/cancel (content-length)
response: HTTP/1.1 413 Payload Too Large | connection: null
rest of body: 63.06 MiB of 64.00 MiB accepted in 43 ms, then complete
next request on the same connection: HTTP/1.1 200 OK

== /partial (content-length)  # reader lock released, stream not cancelled
response: HTTP/1.1 413 Payload Too Large | connection: ["close"]
rest of body: 2.06 MiB of 64.00 MiB accepted in 5003 ms, then timeout
next request on the same connection: n/a
```

## Expected

RFC 9112 §9.3 lets a server either read the whole body or close the connection after the response. Reading the whole body is conformant, but a bounded drain is what makes an early rejection useful: Go's `net/http` drains up to 256 KiB (`maxPostHandlerReadBytes`) and closes past that, with a short lingering close so the client still gets the response. Something similar here, in particular:

- Cap the drain in `drain_raw_h1_request_body` / `discard_body_with_scratch` (ext/http/http_next.rs, libs/http_h1/conn.rs). Past the cap, send `connection: close` and close.
- When the connection is already marked to close (untouched body), do not read the body to the end first. Shut down the write side and close after a short grace period instead.

## Actual

- Untouched body: `connection: close` is sent, but the remaining 64 MiB is read in full before the socket closes.
- Partly read and cancelled body: the remaining 64 MiB is read in full and the connection is kept alive.
- Partly read body whose reader lock was released without cancelling: the response carries `connection: close`, the server stops reading, and the socket is neither read nor closed for as long as the repro waits (the connection has been handed to the JS reader and is only closed when the stream is dropped or collected). This path is probably rare in practice but worth noting since it leaves the upload hanging.

