**CR/LF in a proxied request's target host/port reaches the proxy request unchecked**

When a `node:http`/`node:https` request goes through an env-configured proxy (`NODE_USE_ENV_PROXY` / `HTTP_PROXY`), `ClientRequest` writes the caller's target `host`/`port` into the request sent to the proxy: the absolute-form request line for an http proxy, and the `CONNECT` line plus `Host` header for an https one. The proxy setup only used `checkInvalidHeaderChar` to decide whether to skip the target permission check, then stored the raw `host`/`port`, trusting the later `Host` header setter to reject CR/LF. That setter is skipped when `setHost: false` is passed or the caller supplies its own `Host` header, so a value like `host: "example.com\r\nX-Injected: 1"` was injected verbatim into the request the client sends to the proxy (header injection / request smuggling). Node rejects it with `ERR_INVALID_CHAR`; the fix does the same at the proxy boundary before the value is stored or written, and valid targets are unaffected.

Repro (before this fix the first line prints `no error`):

```js
// NODE_USE_ENV_PROXY=1 HTTP_PROXY=<url> deno run --allow-all r.mjs
import http from "node:http";
for (const host of ["example.com\r\nX-Injected: 1", "ok.example"]) {
  try {
  http.request({ host, port: 80, setHost: false }).destroy();
  console.log("no error");
  } catch (e) {
  console.log(e.code);
  }
}
```

Covered by a new spec test in `tests/specs/node/http_proxy_crlf_target`.

